course: politics
lesson: 32
title: Bots, troll farms and deepfakes in elections
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A bot is a computer program that posts by itself while pretending to be a person, and a troll farm is an office of people paid to post under fake names.
explain: Picture a school vote where one kid wears ten different hats and votes ten times: it looks like a crowd, but it is one person with many masks, and that is what a swarm of fake accounts does online.
explain: A deepfake is a picture, video or voice made with AI to look like a real person doing or saying something they never did; extra fingers or a mouth that does not match the words can give it away, but the fakes keep getting better.
explain: The flip side matters too: because fakes exist, people caught on a real video can shout "it's AI!", so "it's fake" is also a claim to check.
explain: You can still check: who first shared it, does trusted news have it, and what does the official election office say?
fact: A bot is a computer program that posts by itself as if it were a person; hundreds of almost identical comments in one minute from accounts with no photos are a warning sign.
fact: It was reported by US investigators that a group in Russia called the Internet Research Agency ran many fake accounts pretending to be Americans around the 2016 election; such a group is often called a troll farm.
fact: People disagree about how much that meddling changed: some say it reached millions, others say it is hard to show it changed many votes.
fact: News reports said that in New Hampshire in 2024 a robocall used an AI copy of President Biden's voice to tell people not to vote in a primary, and that the people behind it were fined.
fact: AI can make pictures of real people doing things they never did; strange hands, blurry writing and odd backgrounds are clues, but clues will not always work.
fact: A deepfake is a fake video, picture or voice made with AI; lips slightly out of time, a too-smooth face or a flat voice can be signs.
fact: The liar's dividend means people use the existence of fakes to deny things that really happened.
fact: An account that posts day and night only about politics, with a stock photo and a new account, may be a bot, but no single sign proves it.
fact: A message saying voting day has moved is a common trick; the real date and place come from the official election office.
fact: Astroturfing means making a fake crowd look like real support; many accounts made last week that follow nobody are a warning sign.
fact: Some cameras and apps are starting to add content labels that show whether a picture was made or changed with AI, though this is new.
fact: Words in this lesson: "rizz" means charm; "delulu" means believing something that is not real; "salty" means annoyed; "wicked" in New England means very; "bare" in London means lots of.
exercise: What is a bot? | A computer program that posts by itself as if it were a person | A real person who posts a lot about sport, often many times a day and night | A news reporter who works from home | A robot that counts votes on election day
exercise: Hundreds of almost the same comments appear in one minute from accounts with no photos. What might they be? | Bots or fake accounts | A crowd of real neighbours who all agree | A newspaper correction | A fact-check by a news outlet
exercise: What is a troll farm? | An office of people paid to post online under fake names | A farm where trolls from fairy tales live under bridges and scare goats away | A group of fact-checkers checking claims | A club for people who love video games
exercise: What did US investigators report about 2016? | A group in Russia ran fake accounts pretending to be Americans | Every American account online was a bot, and no real people posted at all that year | Nobody tried to meddle in the election at all | All the votes in the election were counted by robots
exercise: What did news reports say about a robocall in New Hampshire in 2024? | It used an AI copy of President Biden's voice telling people not to vote | It was a real message from President Biden about voting, recorded in the White House that morning | It told people the right place and time to vote | It was a joke that nobody believed at all
exercise: A picture of a world leader dancing in a costume has six fingers on a hand. What does that suggest? | It was probably made with AI | The leader really has six fingers | The camera was very old and broken | It is a photo from a history book
exercise: What is a deepfake? | A fake video, picture or voice made with AI to look like a real person | A real video filmed deep under the sea | A video that has been checked and corrected by a news outlet before it was shown again | A cartoon that everyone knows is a cartoon
exercise: A politician's mouth does not quite match the words in a shocking video. What is the best check? | See whether trusted news outlets have the same clip | Share it fast before it gets deleted | Believe it, because you saw it with your own eyes and eyes never lie | Turn up the volume to hear it better
exercise: What is the liar's dividend? | When people use the existence of fakes to deny true things | When liars get paid a share of the profits by their bank | When fakes are always caught at once, so that lying never pays | When a news outlet prints a correction and pays the reader back
exercise: An account says "proud mum from Ohio" but posts every few minutes all day and night only about politics. What might it be? | A bot or fake account, though no single sign proves it | Definitely a real mum, because it says so in the profile and mums are busy | A government election office | A public broadcaster like the BBC
exercise: A message says "Voting day has moved to Wednesday". Where do you check? | The official election office or its website | The account that sent the message | A friend who also got the message | A comment under the same post
exercise: What is astroturfing? | Making a fake crowd look like real support | Laying plastic grass on a football pitch | Counting votes by hand in a big hall | Asking everyone in the class to vote
exercise: Most accounts sharing "Thousands are leaving this party!" were made last week and follow nobody. What does that suggest? | It may be a fake crowd made of new accounts | Thousands really left the party last week | New accounts are always the most honest | The party has gone out of business
exercise: If AI can fake anything, how can we still check? | Ask who first shared it, whether trusted news has it, whether official sites agree | We cannot check anything any more, so believe nothing at all and stop reading the news | Believe whatever has the most likes | Believe whatever looks the most real
exercise: What are content labels? | Notes showing that a picture was made or changed with AI | Price stickers on food in a supermarket | Names written on school bags | Stickers that make a photo look older
exercise: Why do people disagree about how much the 2016 fake accounts mattered? | Some say they reached millions, others say it is hard to show votes changed | Everyone agrees they changed every single vote in every single state, so the result was decided by them | Everyone agrees they reached nobody at all | Nobody has ever heard of the fake accounts
test: "lol 300 comments all saying the exact same thing in one minute, that guy's got mad rizz, everyone loves him" — what is more likely? | Many may be bots, and copies of one comment are not many people | He really has 300 fans who type exactly alike, because they all went to the same school | Comments posted together are always from real friends | It proves he will win the election
test: In Boston: "Wicked weird, Mom got a call in the President's voice saying don't vote." What is the best thing to do? | Check the vote on the official election office's site | Stay home, because the voice sounded real | Call back and ask the voice if it is true | Tell everyone not to vote either
test: "delulu much? that pic's real, I can see it with my own eyes" — the picture has six fingers on one hand. Who is more likely right? | The friend who doubts it, because odd hands are a clue to AI | The one who believes it, because eyes never lie | Neither, because pictures cannot be made with AI | Both, because a picture can be real and fake at once, depending on who looks at it
test: What would happen if everyone believed any video could be fake? | People caught on real videos could deny what they did | Nobody could ever tell a lie again, because nobody would believe anything they did not see in person | All deepfakes would disappear overnight | Every video would be checked automatically
test: In London: "Bare accounts posting all day and night about the election, all with the same stock photo, bruv." What should you think? | They may be bots, so do not treat them as real people agreeing | They must be a big group of very keen voters who all use the same photo because they are in a club | They are the official election office | They prove most people agree with them
test: A boy says, "Investigators said for sure that the fake accounts changed every vote in 2016." What is the better version? | It was reported that fake accounts ran; how much they changed is disputed | He is right, every vote was changed by them | Investigators said no fake accounts existed at all, and that the stories about them were made up | The accounts were run by the voters themselves
test: "lowkey salty, my teacher said the politician video was real and I said it's AI" — the clip is on three trusted news sites with the full speech. Who is more likely right? | The teacher, because trusted outlets have the full speech | The pupil, because every video now is AI | Nobody, because no video can ever be checked | The pupil, because the teacher is a grown-up and grown-ups cannot spot AI videos
test: In a cartoon, a villain uses a machine to copy the hero's voice and tells the town to stay home. Which real thing is like that? | A voice-clone robocall telling people not to vote | A fact-checker rating a claim as true | A newspaper printing a correction | A class voting by secret ballot
test: "Voting's moved to Wednesday, my mate's cousin sent it, trust." What is the safest step? | Check the official election office before believing it | Believe it, because a cousin of a mate sent it and he knows stuff | Forward it to everyone in the family, just in case it is true | Go on both days, just to be safe, and vote twice if you can
test: Which is true about clues like extra fingers or blurry writing? | They help, but AI keeps improving so they will not always work | They always catch every single fake, forever, however good the AI gets | They only ever appear in real camera photos, never in AI pictures | They prove a picture is real, because AI always draws hands right
test: Fill the gap: a crowd online can be a few people with many ___ | masks | ballots | cameras | parties
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a "bot" in the context of online platforms? | A computer program which posts automatically whilst purporting to be a person | A natural person who posts with considerable frequency, typically upon matters of sport or entertainment | A journalist employed by a public broadcaster | A device employed in the counting of ballots
exam: Identify the phenomenon whereby the existence of fabricated media is invoked in order to deny authentic recordings. | The liar's dividend | Astroturfing | Satire | A correction
exam: Whereas a communication asserts that polling day has been altered, select the source most appropriate for verification. | The official body responsible for the administration of the election | The account from which the communication originated | Any acquaintance who received the same communication | The comments appended to the communication
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning foreign accounts at the 2016 United States election. | It was reported that such accounts operated, whereas the extent of their effect remains disputed | It was established that such accounts altered every vote cast | It was established that no such accounts existed | It was reported that such accounts were operated by the electoral authorities in each of the fifty states
